# Integrate, do not strand

These repos have no external consumers. Branches, PRs, and submodules
exist to keep customers isolated and work organized — not as review
gates or as somewhere to leave things. Work that sits on a branch is
not done, and it decays: the parent repo's symlinks break, submodule
pointers drift, and the next engagement re-solves the same problem.

Finish the loop every time:

1. Land the submodule change first (merge it), then move the parent
   repo's pointer to the merged commit in the same session.
2. Verify the parent still composes after the merge — symlinks
   resolve, the values stack renders.
3. Generalize what is reusable up into the shared library
   (`jfrog-helm-values`) and keep only the site-specific remainder
   here. A fix worth making once for this customer is usually worth
   making for the next.
4. Prove behavior, do not assert it. Render before and after and diff
   the output.

Customer-specific things stay customer-specific: URLs, secret refs,
sizing deltas, TLS. Everything else belongs upstream.

```text
# BAD
Open a PR for the label, leave it unmerged, and hand back a customer
repo whose helm/ symlinks only resolve while the submodule sits on a
feature branch.

# GOOD
Merge the label, point the submodule at the merged commit, confirm the
stack still renders, and commit the customer repo against it.
```
